media: h713-hdmirx: report the colour the firmware read out of the AVI

The driver has answered V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709 and
V4L2_QUANTIZATION_LIM_RANGE for every source since the format was first
filled in. That is right for 720p and everything above it and wrong for
every SD source, for every source that sends its own colorimetry, and for
every full-range one - and the information to be right with has been sitting
unread in the firmware's 44 byte record all along.

Where each field comes from, with the report section that proves it:

  colorspace / ycbcr_enc   record color_space (kHalColorSpace_*), which
        GetSignalColorSpace@0x8B133D2C produces from the colorimetry word of
        the stored AVI InfoFrame - C of DB2 and EC of DB3 (AP3i sections 3
        and 4). The mapping, one row per enumerator:
            0 BT601         -> SMPTE170M
            1 BT709         -> REC709
            2 BT2020_NCLYCC -> BT2020
            3 BT2020_CLYCC  -> BT2020 + YCBCR_ENC_BT2020_CONST_LUM
            4 xvYCC         -> REC709 + YCBCR_ENC_XV709
            5 RGB           -> SRGB
        The two low codes are swapped against the firmware's internal
        0x4400xx numbering, which is the obvious way to get this wrong
        (AP3i section 2). Rows 4 and 5 cannot occur: xvYCC and sYCC fold
        into plain 601/709 during the AVI parse and RGB is never produced
        here. A value outside the table keeps REC709, as before.
        Where the AVI carries no colorimetry at all the firmware has already
        decided for us: BT2020_NCLYCC on a source that really sends HDR
        signalling, BT709 on a PC timing, BT601 for exactly eleven SD signal
        IDs, BT709 otherwise (AP3k section 6, AP3q section 5). So this
        changes the report only for SD sources and for sources that do send
        colorimetry - on the six verified HY310 modes it reports what it
        reported before.

  quantization             record b_full_range, which is GetQuantRange@
        0x8B133F24: the HDMI default rule, YCbCr limited unless YQ says
        otherwise, RGB with Q = default full only on a PC timing (AP3i
        section 5).

  V4L2_DV_FL_IS_CE_VIDEO   record b_dvi_mode, the firmware's own "this is
        not HDMI video" verdict - the port's HDMI/DVI mode, raised for
        fourteen XGA timings by the timing bitmask and overwritten by the
        vendor's PC-mode config (AP3i sections 6, 9 and 10). It is the one
        thing about the timing that a preset cannot know, so it decides the
        flag rather than the preset that was matched.

  xfer_func                left DEFAULT, deliberately. V4L2 has no HDR
        metadata for a capture device, and an HDR transfer function would be
        wrong in the other direction anyway: the firmware tone maps an HDR
        source onto the panel before the capture ever sees it (AP3q sections
        6 to 8), so what lands in the ring is already mapped.

The three values are cached in struct h713_hdmirx and filled only from a
genuine HDMI record, never from the record our own descriptor republish
leaves behind (0136i's own_record test). G_FMT therefore costs no cpu_comm
read, and a moment without a record keeps the last honest answer instead of
falling back to a guess.

The status file gains two lines a human can read, "colour:" and "hdr:". The
HDR one names the kHalHdrScheme_* the record carries and says what the two
surprising values mean: Sdr can also be plain HDR10, because the firmware
has no pair for that internal code and mislabels it towards the ARM (AP3k
section 11) while tone mapping it correctly all the same (AP3q section 9);
and DbVision, on this firmware, means the Dolby Vision low-latency VSIF with
PB4 bits [1:0] = 11b, because the EDR route that shares the value is never
taken - nothing in display.bin ever writes the byte it needs (AP3q sections
2, 3 and 4). Nothing on our side acts on hdr_mode, and nothing should.

Nothing in the AVI beyond those fields belongs in what we report: of the ten
fields the firmware parses it reads four, we already have all four through
the record, and pixel repetition acts on the capture before INCAP, so
reporting it would double-count (AP3k sections 7 to 9).

h713-tv, in the same commit: `ctl status` gains a "colour" line from G_FMT
and forwards the driver's two new status lines.

Source: umbau/re-apps/AP3i (12), AP3k (13), AP3q (11), plus their REVIEW.md.
Follow-up to 0136i; 0136f - 0136m stay byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -150,6 +150,42 @@ struct h713_hdmirx_signal_info {
 
 static_assert(sizeof(struct h713_hdmirx_signal_info) == 44);
 
+/*
+ * What one kHalColorSpace_* - the record's color_space - means in V4L2 terms,
+ * with the firmware's own reading of it for the status file.
+ *
+ * It is the firmware's verdict on the AVI InfoFrame, not a register:
+ * GetSignalColorSpace@0x8B133D2C indexes its own table with the colorimetry
+ * word (C and EC of DB2/DB3), and only a colorimetry of 0 falls back - to
+ * BT2020_NCLYCC on an HDR source, to BT709 on a PC timing, to BT601 for
+ * eleven SD signal IDs, to BT709 otherwise (AP3i 3 and 4, AP3k 6). xvYCC and
+ * sYCC fold into plain 601/709 on the way, so rows 4 and 5 cannot occur.
+ */
+struct h713_hdmirx_colour {
+	u32 colorspace;
+	u32 ycbcr_enc;
+	const char *name;
+};
+
+static const struct h713_hdmirx_colour h713_hdmirx_colour_map[] = {
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_SMPTE170M, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT, "BT.601" },
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT, "BT.709" },
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_BT2020, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT, "BT.2020 NCL" },
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_BT2020, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_BT2020_CONST_LUM, "BT.2020 CL" },
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_XV709, "xvYCC" },
+	{ V4L2_COLORSPACE_SRGB, V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT, "RGB" },
+};
+
+/*
+ * kHalHdrScheme_*, the record's hdr_mode. Only 0, 2, 5 and 6 are reachable:
+ * 1 (Hdr10) is paired with an internal code GetHdrWorkMode never returns, so
+ * plain HDR10 arrives here as Sdr (AP3k 11) although the firmware tone maps
+ * it correctly all the same (AP3q 9); 3 and 4 have a number and no name.
+ */
+static const char * const h713_hdmirx_hdr_scheme[] = {
+	"Sdr", "Hdr10", "Hdr10Plus", NULL, NULL, "Hlg", "DbVision",
+};
+
 /*
  * kHalSignalID_* as far as the name table in display.bin (0x8b14adc0) is
  * decoded: the first three are the ones that mean "nothing to report". The
@@ -1142,6 +1178,11 @@ struct h713_hdmirx {
 	 * detect_timings(), which the ioctl lock serialises.
 	 */
 	u32 unlisted_active;
+
+	/* what the format says about colour, from the last genuine record */
+	u32 fmt_colorspace;
+	u32 fmt_quantization;
+	u32 fmt_ycbcr_enc;
 	struct mutex sig_lock;
 	void __iomem *sig_map;
 	phys_addr_t sig_map_base;
@@ -3344,6 +3385,33 @@ static void h713_hdmirx_ring_geometry(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 			     how);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The colour the format reports, from the record's own two fields.
+ * b_full_range is GetQuantRange@0x8B133F24, the HDMI default rule: YCbCr is
+ * limited unless YQ says otherwise, RGB with Q = default is full only on a PC
+ * timing (AP3i 5). The driver said REC709 and LIM_RANGE for every source
+ * until now - right for 720p and up, wrong for SD and for anything that does
+ * send colorimetry (AP3k 6). Only called with a genuine HDMI record.
+ */
+static void h713_hdmirx_fill_colour(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
+				    const struct h713_hdmirx_signal_info *sig)
+{
+	if (sig->color_space < ARRAY_SIZE(h713_hdmirx_colour_map)) {
+		const struct h713_hdmirx_colour *c;
+
+		c = &h713_hdmirx_colour_map[sig->color_space];
+		rx->fmt_colorspace = c->colorspace;
+		rx->fmt_ycbcr_enc = c->ycbcr_enc;
+	} else {
+		/* kHalColorSpace_Max or a value no name table covers */
+		rx->fmt_colorspace = V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709;
+		rx->fmt_ycbcr_enc = V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT;
+	}
+
+	rx->fmt_quantization = sig->b_full_range ? V4L2_QUANTIZATION_FULL_RANGE
+						 : V4L2_QUANTIZATION_LIM_RANGE;
+}
+
 /*
  * A source the receiver will not lock, said once.
  *
@@ -3567,6 +3635,16 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 
 	/* have_sig is by now exactly "a genuine HDMI record" (own_record above) */
 	if (have_sig) {
+		h713_hdmirx_fill_colour(rx, &sig);
+		/*
+		 * b_dvi_mode is the firmware's own "this is not HDMI video"
+		 * verdict (AP3i 6, 9 and 10) and the one thing about the
+		 * timing a preset cannot know, so it decides the CE flag.
+		 */
+		if (sig.b_dvi_mode)
+			bt->flags &= ~V4L2_DV_FL_IS_CE_VIDEO;
+		else
+			bt->flags |= V4L2_DV_FL_IS_CE_VIDEO;
 		rx->lock_timings = *t;
 		rx->lock_active = active;
 		rx->lock_total = total;
@@ -3627,8 +3705,13 @@ static void h713_hdmirx_fill_fmt(struct h713_hdmirx *rx,
 		pix->plane_fmt[i].sizeimage = rx->stride * pix->height;
 	}
 	pix->field = V4L2_FIELD_NONE;
-	pix->colorspace = V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709;
-	pix->quantization = V4L2_QUANTIZATION_LIM_RANGE;
+	/*
+	 * xfer_func stays DEFAULT on purpose: the firmware tone maps an HDR
+	 * source onto the panel before the capture sees it (AP3q 11 D1).
+	 */
+	pix->colorspace = rx->fmt_colorspace;
+	pix->quantization = rx->fmt_quantization;
+	pix->ycbcr_enc = rx->fmt_ycbcr_enc;
 }
 
 static int h713_hdmirx_enum_fmt(struct file *file, void *priv,
@@ -4055,6 +4138,17 @@ static const struct v4l2_file_operations h713_hdmirx_fops = {
  * be decoded, and its raw words have to come from somewhere.
  * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
 
+/* What the two surprises in hdr_mode mean, for a reader without the reports. */
+static const char *h713_hdmirx_hdr_note(u32 hdr_mode)
+{
+	if (hdr_mode == 0)
+		return " -- Sdr here can also be plain HDR10: the firmware has no pair for that internal code and mislabels it towards the ARM, and tone maps it correctly anyway";
+	if (hdr_mode == 6)
+		return " -- on this firmware DbVision means the Dolby Vision low-latency VSIF (PB4 bits 1:0 = 11b); the EDR route that shares this value is never taken";
+
+	return "";
+}
+
 static void h713_hdmirx_show_signal_info(struct seq_file *s,
 					 struct h713_hdmirx *rx)
 {
@@ -4083,6 +4177,26 @@ static void h713_hdmirx_show_signal_info(struct seq_file *s,
 		   sig.b_interlace ? "interlaced" : "progressive",
 		   sig.h_size, sig.v_size, sig.color_format, sig.color_space,
 		   sig.hdr_mode, sig.b_full_range, sig.b_dvi_mode);
+
+	/*
+	 * The same record in words, so what G_FMT reports can be checked
+	 * against what the firmware said. b_interlace is a set lookup on the
+	 * matched signal ID, not a register or an AVI bit (AP3k 5).
+	 */
+	seq_printf(s,
+		   "colour:       %s, %s range, %s, %s source (color_space %u, b_full_range %u, b_interlace %u, b_dvi_mode %u)\n",
+		   sig.color_space < ARRAY_SIZE(h713_hdmirx_colour_map) ?
+			h713_hdmirx_colour_map[sig.color_space].name : "unknown",
+		   sig.b_full_range ? "full" : "limited",
+		   sig.b_interlace ? "interlaced" : "progressive",
+		   sig.b_dvi_mode ? "DVI/PC" : "HDMI",
+		   sig.color_space, sig.b_full_range, sig.b_interlace,
+		   sig.b_dvi_mode);
+	seq_printf(s, "hdr:          %s (hdr_mode %u)%s\n",
+		   sig.hdr_mode < ARRAY_SIZE(h713_hdmirx_hdr_scheme) &&
+		   h713_hdmirx_hdr_scheme[sig.hdr_mode] ?
+			h713_hdmirx_hdr_scheme[sig.hdr_mode] : "unnamed",
+		   sig.hdr_mode, h713_hdmirx_hdr_note(sig.hdr_mode));
 }
 
 /*
@@ -4344,6 +4458,10 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&rx->rearm_retry_work, h713_hdmirx_rearm_retry_work);
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&rx->audio_work, h713_hdmirx_audio_work);
 	h713_hdmirx_set_timings(rx, &h713_hdmirx_timings, &h713_hdmirx_ring_default);
+	/* until the first genuine record: what the driver reported all along */
+	rx->fmt_colorspace = V4L2_COLORSPACE_REC709;
+	rx->fmt_quantization = V4L2_QUANTIZATION_LIM_RANGE;
+	rx->fmt_ycbcr_enc = V4L2_YCBCR_ENC_DEFAULT;
 	rx->last_slot = -1;
 	mutex_init(&rx->lock);
 	mutex_init(&rx->cb_lock);
